  "query_preview": "..\n\nSoutheast Michigan will remain along the northern periphery of very \nstrong H5 600 dam ridge that will retrograde from the Southeast U.S. \nto Texas by Friday. Quick progression of deep northern Canada \nmidlevel trough combined with the expanse of upper level confluence \nover portions of the Great Lakes will support surface anticyclone \nhere locally and cause the frontal zone and any instability to be \nsuppressed to the south today. Despite the instability to the south, \nplan view perspective of thermal progs show a fairly significant \nwave of 850-600mb warm advection directly through Lower Michigan \nbetween 06-18z this morning. Will see some upstream convection from \nIowa track eastward this morning and at least approach Southeast \nMichigan. Could see some light precipitation or virga survive the \neastward trek but not expecting much in the way of rainfall amounts. \nPersistent clouds today are expected to have some impact on \ntemperatures which will be in the middle 70s, at or a couple of \ndegrees above normal.\n\nRetrogression of the high and deep column ridging will allow for \nstrong surface ridging over Southeast Michigan Friday. Cooler \nmorning lows with some 40s up north, but right at seasonal averages \nfor low temperatures.\n\nSoutheast US monsoonal moisture is forecasted to circumnavigate/ \nadvect around the large Southeast US upper level ridge Friday and \nSaturday. There has been good model consistency in supporting strong \nsynoptic scale lift from South Dakota Friday eastward into Michigan \non Saturday. Strong convergence streamflowing into a region of very \nstrong right entrance region jet dynamics aloft brings high \nconfidence in a storm track within the vicinity. Main concern is the \npotential for heavy rainfall Saturday. EPS ensemble data highlights \nan interquartile range of 24 hr QPF of .8 to 1.5 inches with higher \nvalues in across the northern cwa including the Tri Cities. PoPs are \nat 90-95% for the Day 3 period, the daytime on Saturday. The \nExcessive Rainfall Outlook has much of Southeast Michigan designated \nas a Slight Risk for Excessive Rainfall Saturday.\n\nFor the latter half of the weekend, indications are that an entrance \nregion to the upper level jet streak will stay in vicinity of \nSoutheast Michigan through at least Monday. Easterly flow combined \nwith differential cold advection in the low levels should result in \nvery stable weather with temperatures below normal. Confidence \ndecreases markedly with regards to precipitation potential by \nTuesday with hard to determine shortwave energy timing and location.",
